=== lesson
id: py-sets
title: Sets
summary: A set is a collection that holds each item only once and has no order. Sets remove duplicates, answer "have I seen this before?", and combine with other sets to find what two groups share or don't share.

=== learn
### A set keeps each item once
A **set** is a collection in which every item appears **at most once**. You write a set with curly brackets, like a dictionary, but with single items instead of key: value pairs:

```python
nums = {3, 1, 3, 2, 1}
print(nums)
print(len(nums))
```

```output
{1, 2, 3}
3
```

You typed five numbers, but the set kept only three. The second 3 and the second 1 were dropped, because the set already had them. Duplicates are never an error: they quietly disappear.

Notice also that Python printed the items in an order of its own. Step 5 explains why you can't rely on that order.

Sets are the right tool for questions like "which different values are there?" and "have I seen this before?".

### set() and the {} trap
`set(...)` builds a set from a list, or from anything else you can loop over, and drops the duplicates:

```python
rolls = [6, 2, 6, 6, 1]
print(set(rolls))
print(len(set(rolls)))
```

```output
{1, 2, 6}
3
```

To make an **empty** set, write `set()`. You can't use `{}` for that: dictionaries had curly brackets first, so `{}` means an empty **dictionary**.

```python
print(type({}))
print(type(set()))
print(set())
```

```output
<class 'dict'>
<class 'set'>
set()
```

Python even prints an empty set as `set()`, so it can't be mistaken for `{}`.

| You write | You get |
| --- | --- |
| `{1, 2}` | a set with two items |
| `{"a": 1}` | a dictionary, because the colon makes a pair |
| `{}` | an empty dictionary |
| `set()` | an empty set |

### Adding and checking: add() and in
`s.add(x)` puts `x` into the set. Adding an item that is already there changes nothing: no error, and no second copy. `x in s` checks whether `x` is in the set, and `x not in s` checks that it isn't.

```python
seen = set()
seen.add(5)
seen.add(8)
seen.add(5)
print(len(seen))
print(5 in seen)
print(7 in seen)
```

```output
2
True
False
```

The third `add` did nothing, because 5 was already in the set.

A set finds an item the same way a dictionary finds a key, using its hash, so `in` stays quick even when the set holds a million items. A list has to check its items one by one.

> Sets use `.add()`, not `.append()`. "Append" means "put at the end", and a set has no end. `seen.append(5)` stops with `AttributeError: 'set' object has no attribute 'append'`. An **AttributeError** means the value has no method with that name.

### Removing: remove() or discard()
Two methods take an item out of a set. They only behave differently when the item is **missing**:

| Method | Item is in the set | Item is missing |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `s.remove(x)` | removes it | stops with a KeyError |
| `s.discard(x)` | removes it | does nothing |

```python
nums = {1, 2, 3}
nums.discard(2)
nums.discard(9)
print(nums)
nums.remove(9)
```

```output
{1, 3}
Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "main.py", line 5, in <module>
    nums.remove(9)
    ~~~~~~~~~~~^^^
KeyError: 9
```

`discard(2)` removed the 2. `discard(9)` found no 9 and quietly did nothing. `remove(9)` found no 9 either, and stopped the program.

Use `remove` when a missing item would mean a mistake you want to hear about. Use `discard` when "it might not be there" is fine.

### No order, no indexes
A set has no first item and no second item: no positions at all. So square brackets don't work on a set:

```python
nums = {10, 3, 7}
print(nums)
print(nums[0])
```

```output
{10, 3, 7}
Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "main.py", line 3, in <module>
    print(nums[0])
          ~~~~^^^
TypeError: 'set' object is not subscriptable
```

"Not subscriptable" means "can't be used with square brackets".

When Python prints a set, it shows the items in whatever order it happens to store them. Small whole numbers often come out looking sorted, but `{10, 3, 7}` didn't. With strings, real Python can even print a different order each time you run the same program. So never rely on the order of a set.

Because order doesn't matter, two sets are equal (`==`) when they hold the same items: `{1, 2} == {2, 1}` is `True`.

### sorted(): the items as an ordered list
When you need the items in order, use **`sorted()`**. It takes the items, puts them in order (numbers from smallest to largest, strings in alphabetical order) and gives you back a new **list**:

```python
fruits = {"pear", "fig", "apple"}
print(sorted(fruits))
print(sorted({10, 3, 7}))
```

```output
['apple', 'fig', 'pear']
[3, 7, 10]
```

The set itself doesn't change. `sorted()` works on lists too. Unlike `.sort()` from the lists lesson, it leaves the original alone and returns a sorted copy.

You can loop over a set with `for x in fruits:`, but the items come in an unknown order. Loop over `sorted(fruits)` when the order matters.

> This course always prints `sorted(...)` of a set of strings, never the set itself, so that the output is the same every time.

### Removing duplicates from a list
Turning a list into a set is the quickest way to drop its duplicates:

```python
names = ["Bo", "Ada", "Bo", "Cy", "Ada"]
print(len(set(names)))
print(sorted(set(names)))
```

```output
3
['Ada', 'Bo', 'Cy']
```

`len(set(names))` counts the **different** names, and `sorted(set(names))` lists them in alphabetical order.

A set forgets the original order, though. To keep each name's first appearance in its original place, loop with a **seen set**:

```python
unique = []
seen = set()
for name in names:
    if name not in seen:
        seen.add(name)
        unique.append(name)
print(unique)
```

```output
['Bo', 'Ada', 'Cy']
```

A name goes into `unique` only the first time it comes up. After that it's in `seen`, so the `if` skips it. Animation 1 shows every step.

### Union and intersection
Python combines two sets with operators. Each operator builds a **new** set and leaves both originals unchanged.

```python
a = {1, 2, 3, 4}
b = {3, 4, 5}
print(a | b)
print(a & b)
```

```output
{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
{3, 4}
```

- `a | b` is the **union**: every item that is in `a`, in `b`, or in both. The `|` symbol is called a **pipe**. Read `a | b` as "in a *or* in b". 3 and 4 are in both sets, but a set holds them only once.
- `a & b` is the **intersection**: only the items that are in `a` **and** in `b`. Read `&` as "and".

Swapping the two sets doesn't change these results: `b | a` is the same set as `a | b`, and `b & a` is the same as `a & b`.

### Difference: what one set has that the other doesn't
`a - b` is the **difference**: the items of `a` that are **not** in `b`.

```python
a = {1, 2, 3, 4}
b = {3, 4, 5}
print(a - b)
print(b - a)
```

```output
{1, 2}
{5}
```

Here the order **does** matter. `a - b` starts from `a` and takes away everything that is also in `b`. `b - a` starts from `b` and takes away everything that is also in `a`.

A real use, with names:

```python
invited = {"Ada", "Bo", "Cy", "Di"}
replied = {"Bo", "Di"}
print(sorted(invited - replied))
```

```output
['Ada', 'Cy']
```

Ada and Cy were invited but haven't replied, so they need a reminder.

### List, set or dictionary?
|  | list | set | dictionary |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Written as | `[3, 1, 3]` | `{3, 1}` | `{"a": 3}` |
| Duplicates | allowed | dropped | each key once |
| Order | kept, with indexes | none | keys keep the order they were added |
| Look up by | position: `nums[0]` | only `in` | key: `d["a"]` |
| Good for | items in a sequence | unique items, "seen it?" | finding a value by its key |

One more rule that sets share with dictionary keys: set items must be **immutable**. Strings, numbers and tuples are fine, but a list can't go in a set: `{[1, 2]}` stops with `TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'`.

=== keypoints
- A set holds each item at most once: `{3, 1, 3}` has two items. `set(a_list)` drops a list's duplicates.
- `set()` makes an empty set. `{}` makes an empty **dictionary**.
- `s.add(x)` adds an item (nothing happens if it's already there). `x in s` checks whether it's there.
- `s.remove(x)` raises a `KeyError` if `x` is missing; `s.discard(x)` does nothing instead.
- Sets have no order and no indexes, so `s[0]` is a `TypeError`. `sorted(s)` gives the items as a list in order.
- `a | b` (union): in either set. `a & b` (intersection): in both. `a - b` (difference): in `a` but not in `b`.
- Set items must be immutable, like dictionary keys.

=== example: Two class lists
intro: Set operators answer questions about two groups of people. Every set of names is printed with `sorted()`.
```python
monday = {"Ada", "Bo", "Cy", "Di"}
tuesday = {"Cy", "Di", "Ed"}
print(len(monday), "came on Monday")
print("Both days:", sorted(monday & tuesday))
print("Either day:", sorted(monday | tuesday))
print("Only Monday:", sorted(monday - tuesday))
print("Only Tuesday:", sorted(tuesday - monday))
print("Ed" in monday)
```
1-2: Two sets of names: who came to class on Monday, and who came on Tuesday.
3: `len()` counts the items in a set, the same as for a list: 4 people.
4: `&` keeps the people in both sets: Cy and Di. `sorted()` turns the result into a list in alphabetical order.
5: `|` keeps everyone who is in either set. Cy and Di are in both, but they appear only once.
6: `monday - tuesday` starts with Monday's people and takes away anyone who also came on Tuesday: Ada and Bo.
7: The other way round: Tuesday's people minus Monday's. Only Ed is left.
8: `in` checks one item. Ed is not in the Monday set, so this prints False.

=== example: Tidying a list of tags
intro: Remove duplicates with `set()`, change the set with `add` and `discard`, and print it in order with `sorted()`.
```python
tags = ["python", "code", "fun", "code", "python"]
unique = set(tags)
print(len(tags), "tags,", len(unique), "different")
unique.add("loops")
unique.add("fun")
unique.discard("cats")
print(sorted(unique))
for tag in sorted(unique):
    print("#" + tag)
```
1: A list with duplicates: "python" and "code" appear twice each.
2: `set(tags)` keeps one copy of each tag: python, code and fun.
3: The list still has 5 items, but there are only 3 different tags.
4: "loops" is new, so the set grows to 4 items.
5: "fun" is already in the set, so this does nothing: no error, and no second copy.
6: "cats" isn't in the set. `discard` doesn't mind; `remove` would have raised a KeyError.
7: `sorted()` gives the tags as a list in alphabetical order.
8-9: Looping over `sorted(unique)` visits the tags in that same order, every time.

=== animation: Removing duplicates with a seen set
view: set seen
array unique
vars name
intro: Watch the seen set remember every name, while unique keeps each name once, in its original order. The bubbles in a set have no order.
```python
names = ["Bo", "Ada", "Bo", "Cy", "Ada"]
seen = set()
unique = []
for name in names:
    if name not in seen:
        seen.add(name)
        unique.append(name)
print(unique)
```
1: Five names, but only three different ones.
2: seen starts as an empty set. It will remember every name the loop has already met.
3: unique is the list being built: each name once, in the order it first appears.
4: The loop hands out the next name. After the last one, the loop ends.
5: name is {name!r}. Is it not in seen yet? {name not in seen}, so it is {'new: keep it' if name not in seen else 'a repeat: skip it'}.
6: Add '{name}' to seen, so any later copy of it gets skipped...
7: ...and append '{name}' to unique.
8: Done. unique is ['Bo', 'Ada', 'Cy']: every name once, in the order it first appeared.

=== animation: Intersection and difference by hand
view: set both
set only_a
vars x
intro: A loop that sorts every item of a into one of two new sets. It builds the same sets that a & b and a - b give.
```python
a = {1, 2, 3, 4}
b = {3, 4, 5}
both = set()
only_a = set()
for x in a:
    if x in b:
        both.add(x)
    else:
        only_a.add(x)
print(both, only_a)
print(a & b, a - b)
```
1: a holds 1, 2, 3 and 4.
2: b holds 3, 4 and 5.
3: both will collect the items that are in a AND in b.
4: only_a will collect the items of a that are NOT in b.
5: The loop takes the next item of a. A set has no positions, but a loop still visits every item exactly once.
6: x is {x}. Is {x} in b too? {x in b}.
7: Yes: {x} is in both sets, so it goes into both.
9: No: {x} is in a but not in b, so it goes into only_a.
10: Done. both is {both} and only_a is {only_a}.
11: The & and - operators build exactly the same two sets, in one step each.

=== task: predict easy
title: Duplicates vanish
code:
```python
nums = {4, 2, 4, 4, 2}
print(len(nums))
nums.add(2)
nums.add(7)
print(len(nums))
```
wrong[5\n7]: A set holds each item only once. The duplicate 4s and 2s were dropped as soon as the set was made, so it starts with 2 items, not 5. Then only 7 is new.
wrong[5\n6]: A set never keeps duplicates. `{4, 2, 4, 4, 2}` holds just 4 and 2, so its length is 2, and after adding 7 it is 3.
wrong[2\n4]: `nums.add(2)` doesn't add a second 2, because 2 is already in the set. Only `add(7)` adds something, so the length goes from 2 to 3.
hint: How many **different** numbers are inside the curly brackets?
hint: Adding an item that is already in a set changes nothing.
right: The set keeps one copy of each value, so `{4, 2, 4, 4, 2}` holds only 4 and 2: length `2`. Adding 2 again does nothing, and adding 7 makes it `3`.
wrong: A set drops duplicates. The five numbers you typed contain only two different values, 4 and 2, so the first length is `2`. `nums.add(2)` changes nothing because 2 is already there, and `nums.add(7)` adds a new item, so the second length is `3`.

=== task: mcq easy
title: An empty set
prompt: Which of these makes an **empty set**?
- `{}` :: This is the classic trap. Dictionaries had curly brackets first, so `{}` is an empty **dictionary**: `type({})` is `<class 'dict'>`.
* `set()` :: Right. `set()` with nothing inside the brackets makes an empty set, and Python prints it as `set()`.
- `[]` :: Square brackets make a list. `[]` is an empty list, which allows duplicates and has positions.
- `{0}` :: This is a set, but not an empty one: it holds one item, the number 0, so its length is 1.
hint: One of these is a dictionary, one is a list, and one isn't empty.
hint: Curly brackets with nothing inside already mean something else in Python.
right: An empty set has to be written `set()`, because `{}` was already taken by dictionaries. Python prints an empty set as `set()` for the same reason.
wrong: `{}` looks like it should be an empty set, but it is an empty dictionary. The only way to write an empty set is `set()`.

=== task: mcq easy
title: remove or discard?
prompt: What happens when this program runs?
code:
```python
pets = {"cat", "dog"}
pets.discard("fish")
pets.remove("fish")
print(len(pets))
```
- It prints `2` :: `discard` is happy to find no fish, but `remove` is not. The program stops before the `print` line runs.
- It stops with a KeyError on line 2 :: `discard` never raises an error. When the item is missing, it does nothing.
* It stops with a KeyError on line 3 :: Right. `remove` insists that the item is in the set. There is no "fish", so Python stops with `KeyError: 'fish'`.
- It prints `1` :: Neither line can take anything out, because "fish" was never in the set. And line 3 stops the program before anything is printed.
hint: Both methods are asked to take out "fish", which isn't in the set.
hint: One of the two methods does nothing when the item is missing; the other one raises an error.
right: `discard("fish")` finds no fish and does nothing. `remove("fish")` finds no fish and raises `KeyError: 'fish'`, so the program stops on line 3 and never reaches `print`.
wrong: "fish" isn't in the set. `discard` does nothing in that case, so line 2 is fine. `remove` raises a KeyError in that case, so the program stops on line 3, before anything is printed.

=== task: code easy
title: How many different colors?
prompt: `colors` is a list that is already set, and the checks try several lists. Create a variable `different` that holds how many **different** colors the list contains. For `colors = ["red", "blue", "red"]`, `different` is 2.
starter:
```python
# colors is already set before your code runs
# your code here
```
solution:
```python
different = len(set(colors))
```
tests:
- different
cases:
- colors = ["red", "blue", "red"]
- colors = ["green"]
- colors = []
- colors = ["Red", "red", "RED"]
- colors = ["blue", "blue", "blue", "blue"]
fail[1]: "red" appears twice but counts once, so there are 2 different colors. `len(colors)` would give 3: turn the list into a set first, which drops the second "red".
fail[3]: An empty list has no colors, so `different` is 0. `set([])` is an empty set, and its length is 0.
fail[4]: Capital letters count: "Red", "red" and "RED" are three different strings, so `different` is 3. Don't change the case.
fail[5]: Four copies of "blue" are one color, so `different` is 1.
error[NameError]: Spell `colors` and `different` exactly like this.
require[set(]: Use `set()` to drop the duplicates before you count.
hint: A set keeps each item only once.
hint: `set(colors)` drops the duplicates. What gives the number of items in it?
hint: `different = len(set(colors))`
right: `set(colors)` keeps one copy of each color, and `len()` counts what is left. Because "Red" and "red" are different strings, they count as different colors.
wrong: Turn the list into a set, which drops the repeats, then count the set's items: `different = len(set(colors))`.

=== task: predict easy
title: A set from a word
code:
```python
letters = set("banana")
print(len(letters))
print(sorted(letters))
```
wrong[6\n['a', 'a', 'a', 'b', 'n', 'n']]: A set never keeps duplicates. "banana" has six letters, but only three different ones: b, a and n.
wrong[1\n['banana']]: `set()` loops over what you give it. Looping over a string gives one character at a time, so the set holds letters, not the whole word.
wrong[3\n['b', 'a', 'n']]: `sorted()` doesn't keep the order of the word. It puts the letters in alphabetical order, so "a" comes first.
hint: `set(...)` takes the items one at a time, the same way a `for` loop would. What does a loop over "banana" give?
hint: Which **different** letters does "banana" contain?
right: Looping over "banana" gives b, a, n, a, n, a. The set keeps one copy of each, a, b and n, so its length is `3`. `sorted()` returns them as a list in alphabetical order.
wrong: `set("banana")` takes the characters one by one and drops the repeats, leaving three letters: a, b and n. So `len` prints `3`, and `sorted()` prints them as a list in alphabetical order: `['a', 'b', 'n']`.

=== task: mcq easy
title: Sets don't append
prompt: This program stops with `AttributeError: 'set' object has no attribute 'append'`. What should line 2 be instead?
code:
```python
tags = set()
tags.append("new")
print(len(tags))
```
* `tags.add("new")` :: Right. Sets take in items with `.add()`. After this line the set holds "new", and the program prints `1`.
- `tags.insert(0, "new")` :: `insert` puts an item at a position, and a set has no positions. This line gives `AttributeError: 'set' object has no attribute 'insert'`.
- `tags["new"] = True` :: That's how you add a pair to a dictionary. A set has no keys or positions, so this gives `TypeError: 'set' object does not support item assignment`.
- `tags = "new"` :: This throws the set away and stores the string "new" in `tags` instead. The program would print `3`, the number of letters in "new".
hint: Lists append, because a list has an end. Does a set have an end?
hint: The set method that puts in an item has a three-letter name.
right: A set has no order and no end, so it has no `.append()`. `.add()` puts the item in, and `len(tags)` is then 1.
wrong: The set method for putting in an item is `.add()`: `tags.add("new")`. `.append()` and `.insert()` are list methods that depend on positions, and square brackets don't work on a set.

=== task: fill easy
title: Not on the list
prompt: The pet shop only sells the pets in `allowed`. Fill in the blank so that the program prints a message for each pet that is **not** allowed:

```output
Sorry, no snake
Sorry, no frog
```
code:
```python
allowed = {"cat", "dog", "fish"}
for pet in ["dog", "snake", "cat", "frog"]:
    if pet ?? allowed:
        print("Sorry, no", pet)
```
answer: not in
wrong[in]: `pet in allowed` is True for the pets the shop **does** sell, so this prints "Sorry, no dog" and "Sorry, no cat". You want the opposite check.
wrong[!=]: A string is never equal to a set, so `pet != allowed` is True for every pet, and all four get a "Sorry" line. To ask whether something is missing from a set, use `not in`.
hint: The message should appear when the pet is missing from the set.
hint: `x in s` checks that `x` is there. What checks that it isn't?
right: `pet not in allowed` is True only for snake and frog, the pets that aren't in the set, so only they get a message. Checking with `in` or `not in` on a set is quick, however big the set is.
wrong: You need a check that is True when the pet is missing from the set: `pet not in allowed`. Dog and cat are in the set, so they're skipped; snake and frog aren't, so they get a message.

=== task: predict easy
title: Same items, any order
code:
```python
a = {1, 2, 3}
b = {3, 2, 1, 1}
print(a == b)
print(len(b))
print([1, 2] == [2, 1])
```
wrong[False\n4\nFalse]: A set has no order and no duplicates. `b` holds 3, 2 and 1 once each, which are exactly the items of `a`, so `a == b` is True and `len(b)` is 3.
wrong[True\n3\nTrue]: Sets ignore order, but lists don't. A list has positions: `[1, 2]` has 1 first while `[2, 1]` has 2 first, so they're not equal.
wrong[True\n4\nFalse]: The second 1 in `b` is dropped when the set is made, so `b` has 3 items, not 4.
hint: Two sets are equal when they hold the same items. Does the order you typed them in matter?
hint: What happens to the second 1 in `b`?
hint: Lists keep their order. Is `[1, 2]` the same list as `[2, 1]`?
right: `b` drops its second 1 and holds 1, 2 and 3, the same items as `a`, so `a == b` is `True` and `len(b)` is `3`. Lists are different: their order matters, so `[1, 2] == [2, 1]` is `False`.
wrong: Work out what `b` really holds: 3, 2 and 1, once each. Sets compare by their items only, so `a == b` is True, and `len(b)` is 3. Two lists are equal only when they have the same items in the same order, so the last line prints False.

=== task: parsons easy
title: Count the visitors
prompt: A shop writes down a name for every visit. Arrange the lines so that the program prints how many **different** people visited, and their names in alphabetical order:

```output
3 different visitors
['Ada', 'Bo', 'Cy']
```

Not every line is needed.
lines:
```python
visits = ["Ada", "Bo", "Ada", "Cy", "Bo"]
people = set(visits)
print(len(people), "different visitors")
print(sorted(people))
```
distractors:
```python
people = {visits}
print(len(visits), "different visitors")
```
hint: Make the list first, then turn it into a set to drop the repeated names.
hint: Count the set, not the list: the list still has all 5 visits.
hint: `sorted()` gives the names as a list in alphabetical order.
right: `set(visits)` keeps one copy of each name, so `len(people)` is 3. `sorted(people)` turns the set into a list in alphabetical order, so the output is the same every time.
wrong: Order: the list of visits, `people = set(visits)`, then print `len(people)`, then `sorted(people)`. `{visits}` tries to put the whole list inside a set, which fails with `TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'`, and `len(visits)` counts all 5 visits, repeats included.

=== task: multi easy
title: Which lines are safe?
prompt: `s` holds `{5, 6}`. Choose **every** line that runs without an error.
* `s.add(5)` :: Right. 5 is already in the set, so nothing changes, but adding an item a second time is never an error.
- `s.remove(7)` :: `remove` insists that the item is there. 7 isn't, so this stops with `KeyError: 7`.
- `print(s[0])` :: A set has no positions, so square brackets don't work on it: `TypeError: 'set' object is not subscriptable`.
* `print(7 in s)` :: Right. `in` only asks the question. It prints `False`.
* `print(sorted(s)[0])` :: Right. `sorted(s)` gives a **list**, `[5, 6]`, and a list has positions, so `[0]` works and prints `5`.
- `s.add([7])` :: Set items must be immutable, and a list can change: `TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'`. `s.add(7)` would work.
hint: Think about items that are already there, items that are missing, and positions.
hint: A set has no positions, but the list that `sorted()` gives back does. Three lines are safe.
right: Adding a duplicate and asking `in` never fail, and `sorted()` turns the set into a list, which you can index. `remove` fails for a missing item, square brackets fail on a set, and a list can't go inside a set.
wrong: Check each line: `add(5)` changes nothing (fine), `7 in s` gives False (fine), `sorted(s)[0]` indexes a list (fine). `remove(7)` raises a KeyError, `s[0]` raises a TypeError, and adding a list raises a TypeError too.

=== task: fill medium
title: Friends in common
prompt: Ada and Alan want to know which friends they have in common. Fill in the blank so that the program prints exactly:

```output
['Cy', 'Di']
```
code:
```python
ada = {"Bo", "Cy", "Di"}
