```
labels: line 1 | line 2 | line 3 | line 4 | line 5 | line 6 | line 7
answer: 1 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 1 | 2 | 1
wrong[1 | 2 | 3 | 2 | 1 | 2 | 1]: Line 3 adds "red", which is already in the set, so the length stays at 2. A set never holds two copies.
wrong[1 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 1 | 1 | 0]: `"Red"` with a capital R is a different string from `"red"`. Line 6 adds a new item (length 2), and line 7 removes only the lowercase "red", leaving "Red" (length 1).
wrong[1 | 2 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 0]: `discard("green")` on line 4 does nothing, because "green" isn't in the set. So the length stays at 2 there.
