hint: The first time a number appears, it isn't in `seen` yet. Every later time, it is.
right: 4 (new), 7 (new), 4 (repeat, count 1), 1 (new), 7 (repeat, count 2), 4 (repeat, count 3). Adding a number that is already in the set changes nothing, so `seen` ends with 3 items: 1, 4 and 7.
wrong: Trace it: 4 isn't in `seen` (add it), 7 isn't (add it), 4 is (count 1), 1 isn't (add it), 7 is (count 2), 4 is (count 3). Each number is in `seen` once, so `len(seen)` is 3. The output is `3 3`.

