both = a & b
a.add(4)
print(sorted(a | b))
print(sorted(both))
print(sorted(a - b))
```
wrong[[1, 3, 4, 5]\n[3, 4, 5]\n[1]]: `both` was built on line 3, before 4 was added to `a`. An operator makes a new, separate set from the values at that moment, so `both` stays `{3, 5}`.
wrong[[1, 3, 4, 5]\n[3, 5]\n[1, 4]]: 4 is in `b`, so `a - b` takes it away too. Only 1 is in `a` without being in `b`.
wrong[[3, 4, 5]\n[1, 3, 4, 5]\n[1]]: You've swapped union and intersection. `|` gives everything in either set; `&` gives only what's in both.
wrong[[1, 3, 3, 4, 4, 5, 5]\n[3, 5]\n[1]]: A union is still a set, so 3, 4 and 5 appear only once, even though (after `a.add(4)`) all three are in both `a` and `b`.
hint: `both = a & b` builds a brand-new set from what `a` and `b` hold **at that moment**.
hint: After line 4, `a` is `{1, 3, 4, 5}`. Does that change `both`?
hint: For `a - b`, start with `a` and take away everything that is also in `b`.
right: `both` was built on line 3, when `a` was `{1, 3, 5}`, so it holds 3 and 5 and doesn't change later. After `a.add(4)`, the union is `[1, 3, 4, 5]`, and `a - b` leaves only `[1]`, because 3, 4 and 5 are all in `b`.
wrong: Take it line by line. Line 3 builds a new set `both` from 3 and 5, the items in both sets right then. Line 4 changes `a` to `{1, 3, 4, 5}`, but `both` is a separate set and stays `{3, 5}`. The union of `a` and `b` is `{1, 3, 4, 5}`. `a - b` removes 3, 4 and 5 from `a`, leaving 1. `sorted()` prints each one as a list.

=== task: parsons hard
title: Find the repeated words
prompt: Arrange the lines so that the program prints every word that appears more than once, in alphabetical order:

```output
['be', 'to']
```

Use one set for the words seen so far and another for the repeated words. Not every line is needed.
lines:
```python
words = ["to", "be", "or", "not", "to", "be"]
seen = set()
repeated = set()
for word in words:
    if word in seen:
        repeated.add(word)
    else:
        seen.add(word)
print(sorted(repeated))
```
distractors:
```python
seen = {}
